1
00:00:25,815 --> 00:00:31,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I came across a few similes about how one should regard sensual pleasures.

2
00:00:34,367 --> 00:00:47,278
[SPEAKER_01]: And how has a monk seen sensual pleasures in such a way that they have no underlying tendency for desire, affection, infatuation, and passion for sensual pleasures?

3
00:00:48,799 --> 00:00:56,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose there was a pit of glowing coals, deeper than a man's height, filled with glowing coals that neither flamed nor smoked,

4
00:00:57,457 --> 00:01:04,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Then a person would come along who wants to live and doesn't want to die, who wants to be happy and recoils from pain.

5
00:01:04,402 --> 00:01:14,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Then two strong men would grab each arm and drag them towards the pit of glowing coals.

6
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:18,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he would struggle to and fro.

7
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:19,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

8
00:01:20,274 --> 00:01:24,076
[SPEAKER_01]: For that person knows, if I fall in that pit of glowing coals,

9
00:01:24,897 --> 00:01:27,559
[SPEAKER_01]: That will result in my death or deadly pain.

10
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:32,621
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the other simile,

11
00:01:37,817 --> 00:01:48,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And how does a monk awakened to a way of conduct and a way of living that such, when they live in that way, bad, unskillful qualities of desire and grief don't overwhelm him?

12
00:01:50,266 --> 00:02:05,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose a person was to enter a thicket full of thorns that have thorns in front and behind, to the left and right, below and above, so they would go forward mindfully and come back mindfully, thinking, may I not get any thorns?

13
00:02:06,937 --> 00:02:13,103
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same way, whatever in the world seems nice and pleasant is called a thorn in the training of the noble ones.

14
00:02:14,063 --> 00:02:20,930
[SPEAKER_01]: When they understand what a thorn is, they should understand restraint and lack of restraint.

15
00:02:22,731 --> 00:02:22,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

16
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:27,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thorns and burning embers.

17
00:02:31,676 --> 00:02:36,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the main thing to mention there, it's like, like that, so the, the last, the second simile.

18
00:02:37,339 --> 00:02:52,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the discipline of the Noble Ones, means the person has the right view, has understood the Four Noble Truths, they need to regard every pleasure and nice thing as a thorn.

19
00:02:53,847 --> 00:02:54,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And how?

20
00:02:54,848 --> 00:02:55,588
[SPEAKER_00]: What would that mean?

21
00:02:55,628 --> 00:02:57,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that would mean as if you're entering a jungle.

22
00:02:58,249 --> 00:02:59,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to enter a jungle.

23
00:03:00,822 --> 00:03:03,544
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are lots of thorns around, so you have to be very careful.

24
00:03:05,885 --> 00:03:12,409
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point to emphasize there, it's not that you have to learn how to be careful.

25
00:03:13,329 --> 00:03:17,992
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about you have to learn how to regard nice things as thorns.

26
00:03:18,812 --> 00:03:23,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, carefulness, caution, they will come automatically.

27
00:03:23,815 --> 00:03:26,517
[SPEAKER_00]: You will then be careful, because it's dangerous.

28
00:03:27,765 --> 00:03:29,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if people would read...

29
00:03:29,246 --> 00:03:40,892
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure many people would have read that simile and then mind would immediately kind of jump into a conclusion that, okay, so I need to be careful, I need to move carefully, I need to do this carefully.

30
00:03:40,912 --> 00:03:42,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's... that's fine.

31
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,714
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can do that.

32
00:03:44,214 --> 00:03:45,895
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not what the simile refers to.

33
00:03:47,956 --> 00:03:54,560
[SPEAKER_00]: You would... you could try and emulate carefulness, but it won't be the carefulness that will result in fulfilling of the Noble Discipline.

34
00:03:55,459 --> 00:03:55,659
[SPEAKER_00]: i.e.

35
00:03:55,679 --> 00:04:01,023
[SPEAKER_00]: it wouldn't result in arahantship, unless you learn how to see these things as thorns.

36
00:04:01,763 --> 00:04:06,546
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it says, if you see things as thorns, then you understand restraint and lack of restraint.

37
00:04:06,566 --> 00:04:09,488
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you understand being careful and being uncareful.

38
00:04:10,588 --> 00:04:20,735
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all rooted in... it's developing certain perceptions in regard to things that would otherwise be a basis for sensual desire.

39
00:04:22,042 --> 00:04:22,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

40
00:04:23,143 --> 00:04:25,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And same with the glowing pit.

41
00:04:27,245 --> 00:04:29,667
[SPEAKER_00]: The sutta describes, and there are other suttas as well, that talk about it.

42
00:04:29,987 --> 00:04:45,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your work, your understanding, your discernment, your sense restraint, that you are doing at the time, all of the effort you put in, should be not in, I will never make a mistake and I will be always careful and mindful and watch every turn I make.

43
00:04:45,797 --> 00:04:45,997
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

44
00:04:46,157 --> 00:04:51,260
[SPEAKER_00]: It should be towards discerning how to see sensuality as burning embers.

45
00:04:51,820 --> 00:05:03,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, sense-destraint will be effortless, dispassion will be outcome of it, non-suffering, non-attachment will be the result of seeing this thing rightly.

46
00:05:04,526 --> 00:05:09,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the work is to recognize these perceptions of danger and

47
00:05:10,505 --> 00:05:13,609
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly in the first simile, so that man

48
00:05:26,783 --> 00:05:31,325
[SPEAKER_00]: His mind, his intention, want to be anywhere else in the world except in the burning pit.

49
00:05:31,846 --> 00:05:45,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the nice thing that used to be a basis for sensuality manifests in the mind of a noble disciple, if he cultivates the perception of danger in it, as the Buddha would say, if he sees it as thorns and so on, he will not want to have it.

50
00:05:46,233 --> 00:05:54,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if he needs to eat, for example, he will eat as if he has to enter a jungle that's full of thorns that can really mess him up.

51
00:05:55,141 --> 00:05:56,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's very careful.

52
00:05:56,501 --> 00:06:05,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Not careful in terms of the food, specifically what he eats, but careful in how he perceives whatever he eats.

53
00:06:05,863 --> 00:06:10,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I lapsing the perception of thorns and embers and dangers that I have developed?

54
00:06:10,884 --> 00:06:17,866
[SPEAKER_00]: The work is not, I need to work harder, I need to be more mindful of my movements, my thoughts, my intentions, my all of this.

55
00:06:18,286 --> 00:06:23,467
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do that, but you need to work harder in terms of unobscuring

56
00:06:25,488 --> 00:06:29,571
[SPEAKER_00]: The understanding and the correct perception of what sensuality really is.

57
00:06:29,611 --> 00:06:32,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality is, factually, like burning embers, like thorns.

58
00:06:33,413 --> 00:06:36,415
[SPEAKER_00]: If it weren't so, you wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

59
00:06:36,455 --> 00:06:40,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter how much you would try to see burning embers, it's just... it would not be compatible.

60
00:06:41,137 --> 00:06:51,143
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it is actually compatible, if you keep cultivating this perception in regard to it, and working your way through, layer by layer, then you get to see it for what it is.

61
00:06:51,183 --> 00:06:53,185
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... the danger became apparent.

62
00:06:53,865 --> 00:06:56,529
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, you don't need to anymore make the effort to discern it.

63
00:06:56,569 --> 00:06:59,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, when sensuality arises, the danger is in its core.

64
00:07:00,292 --> 00:07:05,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no more confusion, there's no more doubt in regard to the nature of pleasing and nice things in the world.

65
00:07:05,835 --> 00:07:08,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you never see, hear, smell, taste, touch anything pleasing in the world.

66
00:07:09,337 --> 00:07:14,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Just means, whenever anything pleasing is experienced, the mind assumes the attitude of a thorny jungle.

67
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:16,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that.

68
00:07:16,761 --> 00:07:20,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because, oh, quickly, I must assume this attitude now in response to this.

69
00:07:21,583 --> 00:07:21,703
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

70
00:07:21,903 --> 00:07:28,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Attitude of carefulness, cautiousness, is automatic, based on the clarity of your perception of thorn,

71
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:31,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I not seeing the danger then?

72
00:07:31,401 --> 00:07:35,184
[SPEAKER_00]: What's obstructing me from not seeing the danger, which is a fact?

73
00:07:35,224 --> 00:07:37,506
[SPEAKER_00]: That danger is a fact, but I'm not seeing it.

74
00:07:50,919 --> 00:07:57,543
[SPEAKER_00]: You can state how the danger is a fact, but that fact is not true to your experience, because you still have passion.

75
00:07:58,163 --> 00:08:04,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you understood the fact of the danger in sensuality, you would be automatically dispassionate.

76
00:08:04,927 --> 00:08:06,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you use as a measure of your understanding.

77
00:08:06,848 --> 00:08:09,990
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't... because often people talk about this as facts.

78
00:08:11,103 --> 00:08:12,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, sensuality is dangerous.

79
00:08:12,405 --> 00:08:21,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, but are you, now, factually, here and now, free from desire and lust towards anything pleasing in the world because of the fact you just stated?

80
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:24,784
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is no, means that's just an abstract fact.

81
00:08:25,477 --> 00:08:30,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is fine in itself, but often people confuse their own abstract facts for understanding.

82
00:08:31,521 --> 00:08:32,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes, everything is impermanent.

83
00:08:33,002 --> 00:08:34,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, anicca everywhere, yeah.

84
00:08:34,584 --> 00:08:38,206
[SPEAKER_00]: It's clear to me, but I don't even have a right view.

85
00:08:38,426 --> 00:08:41,749
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm certainly not an arahant, but here I am talking about anicca.

86
00:08:41,789 --> 00:08:42,489
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

87
00:08:43,030 --> 00:08:46,512
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understood anicca, even to the basic level, you'd be at least a sotāpanna.

88
00:08:46,552 --> 00:08:47,693
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what makes you a sotāpanna.

89
00:08:49,048 --> 00:08:53,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's fine to have abstract ideas and then, obviously, upgrade them.

90
00:08:53,711 --> 00:08:58,793
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is when abstract ideas are taken as facts that now you don't need to do anything about.

91
00:09:00,474 --> 00:09:10,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this particular instance, it's even... well, the symptom that you have not understood the fact of the danger of centrality is even more in your face.

92
00:09:11,661 --> 00:09:18,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Because multiple times during the day, you're going to be engaging with sensuality, relishing, welcoming, planning, worrying on account of it.

93
00:09:19,385 --> 00:09:24,628
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just in your face, just showing you how not dispassionate you are.

94
00:09:24,668 --> 00:09:25,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Thorn arising.

95
00:09:25,668 --> 00:09:26,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

96
00:09:26,488 --> 00:09:28,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Thorn being recognized, carefulness induced.

97
00:09:30,651 --> 00:09:33,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Embers presented, fighting these men that are dragging you there.

98
00:09:34,806 --> 00:09:35,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Automatic.

99
00:09:35,506 --> 00:09:37,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how to recognize the danger?

100
00:09:38,727 --> 00:09:39,567
[SPEAKER_00]: How to recognize the danger?

101
00:09:39,587 --> 00:09:41,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have to take it on trust first.

102
00:09:43,048 --> 00:09:46,589
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha tells you that sensuality is actually dangerous, not worth clinging to.

103
00:09:47,269 --> 00:09:49,270
[SPEAKER_00]: It completely contradicts your entire experience.

104
00:09:49,910 --> 00:09:50,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have an option now.

105
00:09:50,790 --> 00:09:51,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Dismiss the Buddha.

106
00:09:52,110 --> 00:10:01,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if you had enough intelligence to see that there's a good chance he's right, because of other things I said, and because I am dissatisfied with existence and liability to dukkha and so on, you take it on trust.

107
00:10:02,309 --> 00:10:03,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is unpleasant.

108
00:10:03,270 --> 00:10:07,512
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me then see when pleasant, pleasing things manifest in my experience, in my mind.

109
00:10:07,632 --> 00:10:11,634
[SPEAKER_00]: First and foremost, practice... keep the virtue and practice sense restraint.

110
00:10:12,074 --> 00:10:13,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that needs to be taken on trust initially.

111
00:10:15,323 --> 00:10:24,907
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do that, then you will be able to allow these pleasing things to manifest in your mind without you automatically, impulsively breaking the precepts and acting towards them.

112
00:10:25,527 --> 00:10:32,629
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you allow them to manifest in your mind, then you get to and start contemplating and discerning where, how, to what extent it is dangerous.

113
00:10:33,530 --> 00:10:36,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the peril is there, but it's not apparent.

114
00:10:36,811 --> 00:10:38,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is a trap.

115
00:10:39,012 --> 00:10:44,634
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm entering this corridor, but I've been warned before that this is how the traps look like.

116
00:10:45,900 --> 00:10:52,527
[SPEAKER_00]: I could dismiss the warning, or I could be very careful and understand the trap before I get into it, and thus I don't get into it.

117
00:10:53,108 --> 00:10:58,694
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understand how you're trapped, then I will learn how to walk through this corridor and not be trapped.

118
00:10:59,635 --> 00:11:03,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I don't even know how I'm gonna be trapped, I don't know what to be careful about.

119
00:11:06,367 --> 00:11:16,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sense restraint, virtue, needs to be taken on account of that trust that the Buddha said something, even if that you don't necessarily see, but you accept and trust, but not as a goal in itself.

120
00:11:16,436 --> 00:11:17,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, that's it.

121
00:11:17,156 --> 00:11:17,837
[SPEAKER_00]: I trust the Buddha.

122
00:11:18,898 --> 00:11:27,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that I have accepted my sense restraint on trust, I get to see that in regard to which I need to practice sense restraint.

123
00:11:27,805 --> 00:11:34,251
[SPEAKER_00]: I get to see the central thoughts enduring in my mind, pleasing objects trying to overwhelm me and beset me and so on.

124
00:11:35,591 --> 00:11:40,035
[SPEAKER_00]: And I get to then not act upon it immediately, which means giving in and being trapped.

125
00:11:40,615 --> 00:11:44,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Dismiss them, which means running away and not passing through a corridor that you do need to pass.

126
00:11:45,179 --> 00:11:48,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, when you come back, you will come back carelessly and get trapped again.

127
00:11:49,863 --> 00:11:53,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get to understand where, what that danger is.

128
00:11:53,506 --> 00:11:57,529
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't understand it if you keep denying sensuality or blindly engaging with it.

129
00:11:58,524 --> 00:12:06,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it needs to manifest, pleasing objects, you need to manifest in your mind, and you mustn't act out of it, and you must be able to endure it without acting towards it or losing perspective.

130
00:12:08,429 --> 00:12:09,950
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, look, danger.

131
00:12:10,010 --> 00:12:11,111
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the meaning of danger?

132
00:12:11,251 --> 00:12:13,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Danger means what?

133
00:12:15,173 --> 00:12:17,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you regard danger friendly to you?

134
00:12:18,595 --> 00:12:20,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you regard something dangerous as pleasant?

135
00:12:21,056 --> 00:12:21,196
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

136
00:12:21,913 --> 00:12:23,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a certain implications of danger.

137
00:12:24,395 --> 00:12:25,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it wishes me harm.

138
00:12:26,417 --> 00:12:27,798
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually against me.

139
00:12:27,818 --> 00:12:30,800
[SPEAKER_00]: It's trying to deceive me into this, and it's obviously unpleasant.

140
00:12:31,381 --> 00:12:33,723
[SPEAKER_00]: The most obvious, it's... oh, it's painful.

141
00:12:34,403 --> 00:12:42,810
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these are the implications, then, of this pleasing state of mind, possibility of sensuality, pleasing thoughts that I have, that I'm enduring, not acting out of.

142
00:12:43,070 --> 00:12:44,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm trying to discern the danger.

143
00:12:45,012 --> 00:12:46,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is actually...

144
00:12:47,224 --> 00:12:48,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in my favor.

145
00:12:48,865 --> 00:12:49,546
[SPEAKER_00]: To what extent?

146
00:12:49,786 --> 00:12:50,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

147
00:12:50,306 --> 00:12:51,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplate that direction further.

148
00:12:51,927 --> 00:12:52,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand it.

149
00:12:52,548 --> 00:12:53,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarify it.

150
00:12:54,048 --> 00:12:55,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Remove any doubt in regard to it.

151
00:12:57,611 --> 00:13:09,098
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I can tell you this is exactly what the danger is, but that would become like another fact to the listener who is not free from danger, free from this passion, free from passion.

152
00:13:10,059 --> 00:13:14,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, they will just assume the fact as if they are seeing it in these things.

153
00:13:15,978 --> 00:13:17,599
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that resulting in dispassion?

154
00:13:17,639 --> 00:13:17,819
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

155
00:13:17,839 --> 00:13:21,061
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just got a new piece of information that's currently inspiring for you.

156
00:13:22,242 --> 00:13:31,367
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to have your own effort of investigation of these phenomena that endure on account of you being sensory-strained and not giving in to it when it arises.

157
00:13:31,427 --> 00:13:34,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And the danger will become apparent.

158
00:13:35,009 --> 00:13:37,751
[SPEAKER_00]: The similes from the Sutta that the Buddha used will become apparent.

159
00:13:38,171 --> 00:13:42,433
[SPEAKER_01]: The Sutta says, how does he live in such a way that he's not overwhelmed by desire and grief?

160
00:13:42,673 --> 00:13:42,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

161
00:13:43,234 --> 00:13:45,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will not be overwhelmed

162
00:13:47,032 --> 00:13:49,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, in a way, that's saying also what the danger is.

163
00:13:49,973 --> 00:13:53,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Being completely overwhelmed by desire and grief.

164
00:13:53,335 --> 00:13:54,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, sure, sure.

165
00:13:54,395 --> 00:13:55,156
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, exactly.

166
00:13:55,536 --> 00:14:00,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is this state that I'm enduring, that I'm not giving in to, where is the danger of it?

167
00:14:00,878 --> 00:14:01,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Right here, right now.

168
00:14:02,319 --> 00:14:04,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in ten years, not in ten minutes.

169
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:13,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Danger should be apparent in that thing that's there, pressing me, trying to overwhelm me with desire, or grief, or one way or the other.

170
00:14:15,083 --> 00:14:16,883
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see the whole significance.

171
00:14:17,083 --> 00:14:18,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

172
00:14:18,304 --> 00:14:20,044
[SPEAKER_01]: That pressure, then taking the pressure.

173
00:14:20,064 --> 00:14:23,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nobody denies that there is there is a pleasing aspect in consuming the bait.

174
00:14:24,045 --> 00:14:25,206
[SPEAKER_00]: The bait is very tasty.

175
00:14:25,806 --> 00:14:27,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not to be denied.

176
00:14:27,286 --> 00:14:28,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay, it's ugly.

177
00:14:28,127 --> 00:14:28,547
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not.

178
00:14:28,607 --> 00:14:29,147
[SPEAKER_00]: It's beautiful.

179
00:14:30,345 --> 00:14:42,015
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is a danger hidden in that thing, that if you were to understand correctly, that beauty will be unable to overwhelm your mind with desire, because you will see through it.

180
00:14:43,957 --> 00:14:55,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is that danger that is like burning embers that will kill you, that every time you engage in sensuality, and you have been doing, each time you've been re-exposing yourself to it, completely, ignorantly, unaware of it?

181
00:14:57,071 --> 00:15:01,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And making yourself, because of that, subject to death and suffering every single time.

182
00:15:02,117 --> 00:15:10,026
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the deer herd simile that we always speak about, called the bait.

183
00:15:11,808 --> 00:15:17,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a deer trapper does not lay down bait intending well-being for the deer herd.

184
00:15:18,740 --> 00:15:19,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's exactly what we just said.

185
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:21,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this pleasing thing, the bait, that you don't see as a bait?

186
00:15:22,041 --> 00:15:23,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but this is not for my well-being.

187
00:15:23,762 --> 00:15:25,863
[SPEAKER_01]: The traps are not set for your good.

188
00:15:47,552 --> 00:15:50,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sensuality, then, is not for my good.

189
00:15:51,454 --> 00:16:11,143
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you don't see that, but the work is to see it, to discern it in that sensuality, in that which is beautiful and enticing, and that you obviously, automatically, because by seeing it as beautiful, you're automatically seeing it as friendly, you're automatically seeing it as non-threatening, as harmless, and that needs to change.

190
00:16:12,223 --> 00:16:14,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, but why am I seeing this?

191
00:16:14,204 --> 00:16:16,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even that, you don't know how to see danger, but

192
00:16:17,032 --> 00:16:19,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, question yourself that which you can.

193
00:16:19,794 --> 00:16:32,163
[SPEAKER_00]: You do recognize that sensuality, like, say, eating something agreeable or seeing somebody agreeable, the pleasant aspect of it, you are taking it as harmless.

194
00:16:34,105 --> 00:16:34,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

195
00:16:35,666 --> 00:16:36,627
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your basis?

196
00:16:36,707 --> 00:16:39,689
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your justification for taking that pleasure as harmless?

197
00:16:40,129 --> 00:16:41,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Where did you get it?

198
00:16:41,210 --> 00:16:41,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Who told you you should?

199
00:16:44,575 --> 00:16:49,619
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the basis for feeling absolutely entitled to experience pleasure of the senses?

200
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:53,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Like it was... no, that's the most natural thing.

201
00:16:53,482 --> 00:16:56,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what is the basis for your belief that is the most natural thing?

202
00:16:56,664 --> 00:16:58,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is that basis... where did you get that idea from?

203
00:16:59,267 --> 00:17:00,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You just assumed it.

204
00:17:00,648 --> 00:17:01,248
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of what?

205
00:17:01,389 --> 00:17:02,169
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have a clue.

206
00:17:02,349 --> 00:17:03,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And yet, here you are.

207
00:17:03,810 --> 00:17:05,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Your whole world revolves around it.

208
00:17:05,712 --> 00:17:07,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that you haven't even actually thought through.

209
00:17:08,414 --> 00:17:10,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But why am I regarding this as pleasant?

210
00:17:10,535 --> 00:17:13,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another line of questioning you can apply on that same level.

211
00:17:13,257 --> 00:17:14,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I think this is good for me?

212
00:17:15,098 --> 00:17:15,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I... No.

213
00:17:16,019 --> 00:17:16,119
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

214
00:17:16,139 --> 00:17:16,640
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I think?

215
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:19,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I already take this to be good for me?

216
00:17:19,662 --> 00:17:24,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I already assuming that this is worthy of desire and engagement?

217
00:17:25,066 --> 00:17:26,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I assume that pleasure

218
00:17:28,245 --> 00:17:31,106
[SPEAKER_00]: On that emotional level, he's not my enemy.

219
00:17:33,246 --> 00:17:34,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Millions of questions you can ask.

220
00:17:35,067 --> 00:17:45,929
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have generated the right basis through the sense restraint and virtue, then they'll start to pertain to the actual sensuality that you're experiencing, enduring, subjected to, and not acting towards.

221
00:17:46,409 --> 00:17:52,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's exactly like, oh, the deer herd, the trapper, does not set with a well, with a good intention.

222
00:17:52,891 --> 00:17:53,871
[SPEAKER_00]: He sets the traps

223
00:17:54,892 --> 00:17:59,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to harm whatever he wants to catch, not to, you know, make the life better.

224
00:18:01,416 --> 00:18:05,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And then say, oh, so sensuality is there to trap him.

225
00:18:06,039 --> 00:18:07,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why am I assuming that it's friendly?

226
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:09,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I could do that.

227
00:18:11,282 --> 00:18:20,687
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, by... that's what I mean, like, if contemplating danger is too abstract, well, contemplate that which you can see, which is your attitude towards this which you want.

228
00:18:21,148 --> 00:18:21,708
[SPEAKER_00]: It's wanted.

229
00:18:23,374 --> 00:18:25,175
[SPEAKER_00]: But why is it so wanted?

230
00:18:25,475 --> 00:18:27,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that I can't say no to this?

231
00:18:27,616 --> 00:18:29,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that I'm not in control when desire arises?

232
00:18:30,198 --> 00:18:31,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is this pressure?

233
00:18:31,639 --> 00:18:35,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only choice I have is to give in or grit my teeth and not give in.

234
00:18:36,482 --> 00:18:36,983
[SPEAKER_00]: But why?

235
00:18:37,683 --> 00:18:40,465
[SPEAKER_00]: I do actually want to be... Why can't I just say, go away?

236
00:18:41,886 --> 00:18:42,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Or stay now?

237
00:18:42,807 --> 00:18:44,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I not in that position?

238
00:18:44,268 --> 00:18:47,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I being overwhelmed?

239
00:18:47,670 --> 00:18:48,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressured, yeah.

240
00:18:48,230 --> 00:18:48,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressured by that.

241
00:18:50,311 --> 00:18:55,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't see that as a problem, well, no amount of other Buddha's instruction will make any difference to you.

242
00:18:55,595 --> 00:18:56,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is a problem.

243
00:18:57,617 --> 00:18:59,178
[SPEAKER_00]: The atma is the greatest pleasure.

244
00:18:59,879 --> 00:19:00,980
[SPEAKER_00]: It is overwhelming you.

245
00:19:01,340 --> 00:19:01,901
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it.

246
00:19:01,921 --> 00:19:06,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you get it, you're the luckiest man alive, yeah, that's secondary.

247
00:19:06,204 --> 00:19:10,648
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is, you were completely under its thumb, even before you committed to it.

248
00:19:11,509 --> 00:19:16,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that doesn't bother you, means you're ignoring the most obvious...

249
00:19:17,815 --> 00:19:26,321
[SPEAKER_00]: The hunter comes in, catches you, drags you around, and you just pretend it's where you want to go, while the hunter is there in your face, laughing and torturing you.

250
00:19:32,264 --> 00:19:36,167
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you are enticed, full of desire, towards...

251
00:19:36,267 --> 00:19:36,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Overwhelmed.

252
00:19:37,047 --> 00:19:37,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Overwhelmed.

253
00:19:38,868 --> 00:19:42,130
[SPEAKER_00]: That in itself is... Well, that's the root of the problem.

254
00:19:44,072 --> 00:19:46,053
[SPEAKER_00]: If sensuality would not be like that,

255
00:19:47,787 --> 00:19:52,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It would not require or imply loss of perspective, loss of mindfulness and everything else.

256
00:19:52,772 --> 00:19:53,553
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be wholesome.

257
00:19:53,994 --> 00:19:58,499
[SPEAKER_00]: It wouldn't be underlined by more more greed and more aversion and more pressure.

258
00:20:02,403 --> 00:20:03,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So that the deception is there.

259
00:20:04,845 --> 00:20:07,829
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's... we always compare it to a bait, to a trap.

260
00:20:08,652 --> 00:20:09,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's deceiving.

261
00:20:09,713 --> 00:20:10,975
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not what it seems like.

262
00:20:10,995 --> 00:20:12,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not what it shows itself.

263
00:20:12,376 --> 00:20:13,838
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the enticement.

264
00:20:14,058 --> 00:20:14,939
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enticement.

265
00:20:14,959 --> 00:20:16,601
[SPEAKER_01]: It's saying, you know, it's pulling you towards.

266
00:20:16,641 --> 00:20:17,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Look here.

267
00:20:17,321 --> 00:20:17,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Take me.

268
00:20:17,662 --> 00:20:17,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Take me.

269
00:20:17,862 --> 00:20:18,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Take me.

270
00:20:18,062 --> 00:20:18,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, look, look.

271
00:20:18,763 --> 00:20:21,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Look at this breadcrumbs leading you to the prize.

272
00:20:22,067 --> 00:20:24,369
[SPEAKER_00]: And you contemplate the nature of that setup.

273
00:20:24,669 --> 00:20:27,773
[SPEAKER_00]: You will see the nature of the setup of sensual desire.

274
00:20:28,113 --> 00:20:29,695
[SPEAKER_01]: The simile of the deer herd.

275
00:20:31,354 --> 00:20:38,537
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about four types of contemplatives, four types of monastics.

276
00:20:40,638 --> 00:20:49,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first deer herd eat food heedlessly, by going right amongst the bait that the deer trapper has set, and they get caught.

277
00:20:51,068 --> 00:20:56,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like the first type of mendicants, people who are unmindful and unrestrained.

278
00:20:56,650 --> 00:20:58,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no any sensory strength.

279
00:20:58,391 --> 00:20:58,931
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no virtue.

280
00:20:58,951 --> 00:20:59,251
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no...

281
00:20:59,271 --> 00:21:01,672
[SPEAKER_00]: They just, when desire arises, they just act.

282
00:21:02,052 --> 00:21:02,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

283
00:21:02,452 --> 00:21:03,773
[SPEAKER_00]: By body, by speech, by mind.

284
00:21:03,953 --> 00:21:05,833
[SPEAKER_00]: They just, when the itch is there, they scratch it.

285
00:21:06,213 --> 00:21:06,574
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

286
00:21:06,874 --> 00:21:07,754
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's just normal.

287
00:21:07,854 --> 00:21:08,574
[SPEAKER_01]: That's normal person.

288
00:21:08,594 --> 00:21:09,995
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much everybody, yeah.

289
00:21:11,675 --> 00:21:12,996
[SPEAKER_01]: And the second deer herd,

290
00:21:14,063 --> 00:21:21,848
[SPEAKER_01]: They see what's happened, you know, to the first deer herd, and then they shun the bait, but instead go live in a deep forest.

291
00:21:22,449 --> 00:21:25,631
[SPEAKER_01]: But when that food becomes too scarce, they come back.

292
00:21:25,711 --> 00:21:29,974
[SPEAKER_01]: They become too weak, and then they return to that bait and eat heedlessly and get caught.

293
00:21:30,154 --> 00:21:35,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the the second type of people who, even without the Buddha's teaching, would experience this content.

294
00:21:38,071 --> 00:21:53,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't need to, like, practice Buddhism to see that these desires are fleeting, cannot really be satisfied, are controlling your whole life revolves around it.

295
00:21:53,118 --> 00:21:55,799
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, many people kind of recognize, like, what's the point?

296
00:21:57,520 --> 00:22:01,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So there are some who would then, okay, let me do something about that.

297
00:22:01,742 --> 00:22:04,003
[SPEAKER_00]: But then they shun the bait.

298
00:22:05,362 --> 00:22:24,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they got this desire, the sense desire, the pressure, all in regard to the beautiful objects, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, but they don't understand the root of the pressure, the problem, the lack of... they don't understand that the mistake is yours in misperceiving it as pleasing, not seeing the danger of it.

299
00:22:24,975 --> 00:22:27,477
[SPEAKER_00]: They think the danger is... the problem is in the objects.

300
00:22:28,378 --> 00:22:30,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they shun all the objects.

301
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:34,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they determine that they will never engage with anything pleasing in the world.

302
00:22:36,200 --> 00:22:38,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the comparison to the run on top of a mountain.

303
00:22:38,702 --> 00:22:40,663
[SPEAKER_00]: They would avoid anything that could entice them.

304
00:22:41,964 --> 00:22:48,228
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you go into the level of ascetic practice, where you just deny any object of the senses that could be pleasing.

305
00:22:49,029 --> 00:22:53,412
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem with that, the pleasure, or displeasure, is not defined by the objects.

306
00:22:53,672 --> 00:22:54,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It's defined by your state of mind.

307
00:22:56,034 --> 00:23:03,339
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you can be living on top of a mountain, but if you haven't understood that pleasure is rooted in your mind, your desire,

308
00:23:04,246 --> 00:23:15,700
[SPEAKER_00]: You will then, even things that were before displeasing, you go used to it, and now the mind relishes them, and you start finding pleasure, sensuality, in things that you didn't used to.

309
00:23:16,901 --> 00:23:22,568
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, something else starts to serve as a purpose of a sensual object, because your mind framework is still the same.

310
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:25,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't sustain that.

311
00:23:25,181 --> 00:23:28,943
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot sustain that not engagement with sense objects.

312
00:23:29,783 --> 00:23:32,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, something pleasing will come up.

313
00:23:32,545 --> 00:23:35,967
[SPEAKER_00]: You will see, smell, hear, taste, touch something agreeable.

314
00:23:36,867 --> 00:23:43,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And given that there is no understanding where the root of the desire is, the agreeability of these objects will mean desire in my mind.

315
00:23:44,355 --> 00:23:56,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's that second deer herd, when the grass and food runs scarce, and they run out of it in the mountains, when they do get back to things they need to do, see, hear, smell, taste, touch it, they get trapped.

316
00:23:57,497 --> 00:24:02,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they haven't developed any wisdom in regard to the bait, in regard to the trap.

317
00:24:02,278 --> 00:24:04,898
[SPEAKER_00]: They only saw, it's a trap, let me run the opposite way.

318
00:24:05,579 --> 00:24:10,499
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, they encounter a trap, and there is no knowledge of how to avoid the trap.

319
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:13,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, not eat the bait within the trap.

320
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:20,003
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if, you know, you enter the cage, but you don't touch the bait, you eat everything else, you can leave the cage.

321
00:24:20,304 --> 00:24:23,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara won't see you.

322
00:24:24,146 --> 00:24:26,748
[SPEAKER_01]: So, some people see the danger of overindulging.

323
00:24:27,128 --> 00:24:31,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, people would see the dissatisfaction that comes with sensuality.

324
00:24:31,631 --> 00:24:36,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially if you keep indulging sensuality, dissatisfaction becomes obvious to people.

325
00:24:36,474 --> 00:24:41,277
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what many people turn to religion in general, in spirituality and meditation, not specifically Buddhism.

326
00:24:41,946 --> 00:24:45,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the dissatisfaction is not that, you know, hidden and subtle.

327
00:24:46,587 --> 00:24:47,207
[SPEAKER_00]: People see it.

328
00:24:47,328 --> 00:24:56,274
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, how you go about it, determines whether you still remain within the same domain of the same hunter, or you actually free yourself from that trap.

329
00:24:56,294 --> 00:24:57,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's this...

330
00:24:57,495 --> 00:24:59,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't indulge.

331
00:25:00,557 --> 00:25:03,539
[SPEAKER_01]: You remain restrained some of the time.

332
00:25:04,079 --> 00:25:08,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when things get difficult...

333
00:25:08,583 --> 00:25:11,405
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remain restrained on your own terms, like, you know...

334
00:25:11,745 --> 00:25:12,906
[SPEAKER_00]: But when the pressure is too much,

335
00:25:13,775 --> 00:25:16,476
[SPEAKER_00]: You just... You come down the mountain and you go back to the bait.

336
00:25:16,536 --> 00:25:19,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your whole... say, Monday to Friday... Sure, sure.

337
00:25:19,636 --> 00:25:22,617
[SPEAKER_01]: You have worked hard, restrained, you haven't indulged.

338
00:25:22,777 --> 00:25:22,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

339
00:25:23,937 --> 00:25:28,139
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, Saturday, Sunday... No, it's just... Or you do a retreat.

340
00:25:28,379 --> 00:25:28,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Retreat, yeah.

341
00:25:28,879 --> 00:25:33,120
[SPEAKER_00]: For a month, but then retreat's over and you're back to the...

342
00:25:33,560 --> 00:25:34,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

343
00:25:34,340 --> 00:25:35,220
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do a retreat.

344
00:25:35,240 --> 00:25:35,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

345
00:25:35,620 --> 00:25:42,202
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be moderate and not indulge, but not for the sake of avoiding the bait, but for the sake of understanding the trap.

346
00:25:43,793 --> 00:25:52,141
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, when you can't sustain that environment of the mountain anymore, of that extreme withdrawal, you still won't eat unwarily, you still won't take the bait.

347
00:25:52,481 --> 00:25:55,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the bait is right next to you, you will not take the hook.

348
00:25:56,305 --> 00:25:58,187
[SPEAKER_00]: But for that, you need understanding and wisdom.

349
00:25:59,328 --> 00:26:01,170
[SPEAKER_00]: So yeah, that's exactly that second type.

350
00:26:01,210 --> 00:26:07,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people would revolve around that the first impulse in somebody who sees the problem with sensuality is to shun it.

351
00:26:08,183 --> 00:26:10,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I will never engage with sensuality ever again.

352
00:26:11,645 --> 00:26:14,166
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, that determination, you cannot sustain that.

353
00:26:15,767 --> 00:26:16,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, it will change.

354
00:26:16,808 --> 00:26:22,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, something against your will, pleasing, will arise, and pressure will overwhelm you, just like before.

355
00:26:23,052 --> 00:26:34,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you might try to double down on your asceticism, as giants did, and create all these extra elaborate ways of avoiding, but fundamentally, you are fighting the battle in a completely misplaced field.

356
00:26:34,878 --> 00:26:35,539
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not there.

357
00:26:35,679 --> 00:26:37,700
[SPEAKER_00]: The problems, the root of sensuality, are not there.

358
00:26:39,042 --> 00:26:43,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you need to be sense-restrained, but the sensuality is not in the object you are restrained against.

359
00:26:44,584 --> 00:26:49,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is in the pressure that you cannot endure in your mind.

360
00:26:49,546 --> 00:26:51,887
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would have outlined that as a way of enlightenment.

361
00:26:51,927 --> 00:26:57,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said himself, if there was the extreme practice of asceticism and avoidance in the world, I have done it.

362
00:26:59,029 --> 00:27:00,310
[SPEAKER_00]: To the point he refused to breathe.

363
00:27:01,930 --> 00:27:02,491
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't eat.

364
00:27:03,251 --> 00:27:05,932
[SPEAKER_00]: He was so skinny, he would grab his spine through his stomach.

365
00:27:07,158 --> 00:27:10,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, and none of that actually freed me from sensuality.

366
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:11,360
[SPEAKER_00]: He tried it.

367
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:16,383
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is anybody who can rightly say, I tried all this, I am the one.

368
00:27:16,643 --> 00:27:20,645
[SPEAKER_00]: He tried it for the purpose of freeing himself from that pressure, and it didn't free him.

369
00:27:22,106 --> 00:27:29,730
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is to say for those who didn't even try that level of asceticism, and tell you asceticism is the way to free yourself, but they're not even trying it to that extent.

370
00:27:29,750 --> 00:27:29,830
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

371
00:27:30,231 --> 00:27:34,613
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha also thought he was the way, tried it before he was Buddha,

372
00:27:36,147 --> 00:27:39,970
[SPEAKER_00]: To the absolute utter end, there was no more than that to attempt.

373
00:27:40,450 --> 00:27:43,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So he said, whatever pain to experience, I have experienced.

374
00:27:43,213 --> 00:27:48,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever, on account of endurance, renunciation, asceticism, self-torture, I have experienced.

375
00:27:48,377 --> 00:27:49,357
[SPEAKER_00]: No higher pain than that.

376
00:27:49,738 --> 00:27:51,940
[SPEAKER_00]: And I tell you, it was not a way to enlightenment.

377
00:27:52,040 --> 00:27:53,661
[SPEAKER_00]: It was not a way to free me from desire.

378
00:27:54,642 --> 00:27:58,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when he started looking in other directions.

379
00:28:00,206 --> 00:28:02,468
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's inevitable to resist against debate.

380
00:28:04,362 --> 00:28:11,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know enough, you won't go too far and waste all the effort climbing on top of a mountain, just so to return back to the bait and be re-trapped.

381
00:28:13,326 --> 00:28:15,046
[SPEAKER_01]: You will actually start to understand more.

382
00:28:15,627 --> 00:28:33,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's that... there was that story about Buddha in the past, where these ascetics were having some conversation, but the thing is, there was this practice whereby ascetics would go up into the mountains for six months,

383
00:28:34,152 --> 00:28:46,997
[SPEAKER_01]: And practice extreme asceticism, but then the food over there would run out, and then they would have to come down again, and then get fed by the lay people, good food, get fattened up for another six months, and then go again six months, whatever.

384
00:28:47,977 --> 00:28:49,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, from time to time.

385
00:28:49,437 --> 00:28:50,958
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like that balance.

386
00:28:51,558 --> 00:28:56,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I will indulge, but because I renounce, it's all equal, it's all good.

387
00:28:56,920 --> 00:28:58,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, whether you look at it...

388
00:28:58,441 --> 00:28:58,802
[SPEAKER_01]: It evens out.

389
00:28:58,982 --> 00:28:59,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

390
00:28:59,222 --> 00:29:03,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you look at the renouncing or you look at indulging, you don't see where the problem is.

391
00:29:03,085 --> 00:29:09,770
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see that the problem is in that perception of sensuality, pressure that you're under, not in object or saying no to the object.

392
00:29:10,371 --> 00:29:10,811
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

393
00:29:10,831 --> 00:29:20,499
[SPEAKER_00]: You mustn't be trying to get rid of the thoughts of sensuality in a sense of, like, you shouldn't welcome and entertain them, and you must be with sensory strain beforehand.

394
00:29:22,599 --> 00:29:27,503
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, when the thoughts arise, if you get rid of it, it's like, I don't want to think about the trap.

395
00:29:28,084 --> 00:29:29,285
[SPEAKER_00]: I mustn't think about the trap.

396
00:29:30,026 --> 00:29:31,247
[SPEAKER_00]: But how will you then understand it?

397
00:29:32,468 --> 00:29:34,269
[SPEAKER_00]: How will you understand, then, how you are trapped?

398
00:29:35,290 --> 00:29:38,933
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to allow those thoughts to endure, because in themselves, they are not sensuality.

399
00:29:39,894 --> 00:29:40,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Your lack of restraint.

400
00:29:41,463 --> 00:29:45,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Your action, your choosing to act on account of pressure, that's where sensuality is there.

401
00:29:45,408 --> 00:29:46,829
[SPEAKER_00]: And we have said it countless times.

402
00:29:47,390 --> 00:29:55,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people would just assume, oh, it's this object, so any form of pleasure you get, any form of joy you get on account of things in your life, oh, sensuality.

403
00:29:55,579 --> 00:29:55,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

404
00:29:56,927 --> 00:30:00,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality, as the Buddha said, it's basically not rooted in the beautiful objects in the world.

405
00:30:01,451 --> 00:30:07,816
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the man's desire and lust that he revolves around, that pressured desire, i.e.

406
00:30:08,016 --> 00:30:09,257
[SPEAKER_00]: lust.

407
00:30:09,457 --> 00:30:11,839
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sensuality is in those objects.

408
00:30:11,879 --> 00:30:17,784
[SPEAKER_00]: For another hand, for the Tathāgata, who has removed desire and lust in his thoughts, beautiful objects remain.

409
00:30:17,964 --> 00:30:21,567
[SPEAKER_00]: So he still sees the beauty, perceives the beauty, tastes the beauty, hears the beauty.

410
00:30:22,008 --> 00:30:23,469
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no lust in regard to it.

411
00:30:24,599 --> 00:30:27,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the final deer herd in that same simile.

412
00:30:28,702 --> 00:30:33,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to the third deer herd.

413
00:30:35,526 --> 00:30:40,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the third deer herd make their dwellings near the bait and eat it carefully.

414
00:30:41,350 --> 00:30:41,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

415
00:30:41,950 --> 00:30:47,774
[SPEAKER_01]: But the deer trapper then makes a barrier around the bait so as to see where the deer come in and out.

416
00:30:48,967 --> 00:30:51,749
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he finds the herd's dwelling place.

417
00:30:51,989 --> 00:30:58,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't catch them on account of direct engagement with the bait, but he finds other traces that they left behind carelessly.

418
00:30:58,412 --> 00:31:10,639
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is the same with contemplatives who have given up indulgence in external sense objects and extreme ascetic practice, but have not renounced internally their desire for pleasure.

419
00:31:11,359 --> 00:31:14,241
[SPEAKER_01]: They are physically restrained, but not mentally restrained.

420
00:31:14,541 --> 00:31:18,143
[SPEAKER_01]: They act unwholesomely out of pressure of views.

421
00:31:18,403 --> 00:31:19,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

422
00:31:20,364 --> 00:31:21,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the pressure of views.

423
00:31:21,525 --> 00:31:25,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see that the third group of deer, they saw.

424
00:31:26,087 --> 00:31:27,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Engaging carelessly is a problem.

425
00:31:28,008 --> 00:31:33,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Rise of a mind is a problem, because sooner or later you come back, or you encounter somewhere else, and you get caught, because you haven't understood the bait.

426
00:31:33,811 --> 00:31:37,033
[SPEAKER_00]: What if we stay nearby, study the bait, and don't take the hook?

427
00:31:38,016 --> 00:31:42,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, they don't consider other things that the trapper would see.

428
00:31:42,399 --> 00:31:43,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Footprints, this, that.

429
00:31:43,940 --> 00:31:46,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they don't learn how to be invisible to Mara.

430
00:31:46,902 --> 00:31:48,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he gets and catches them.

431
00:31:48,583 --> 00:31:53,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha compared those, basically, traces that you leave to that...

432
00:31:53,066 --> 00:31:56,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not engaging physically with the desire, with sensuality.

433
00:31:56,629 --> 00:31:57,149
[SPEAKER_00]: You're celibate.

434
00:31:57,169 --> 00:31:58,150
[SPEAKER_00]: You're perfectly restrained.

435
00:31:58,750 --> 00:32:00,911
[SPEAKER_00]: But mentally, you still entertain the same value.

436
00:32:02,893 --> 00:32:14,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you refuse to think about women or coarse sensuality or, you know, breaking the precepts, but you think about other forms for the same purpose.

437
00:32:14,720 --> 00:32:21,865
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, stuff you will eat, how you will not be bothered by displeasing sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, how you will build your little palace protected by your

438
00:32:22,485 --> 00:32:27,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever attendance, how you will never experience any hardship, never experience any elements.

439
00:32:27,449 --> 00:32:30,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all that, all of that becomes the place for your sensuality.

440
00:32:30,411 --> 00:32:35,975
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of that still makes you very much concerned about catering to your senses, to your senses, for pleasure.

441
00:32:37,316 --> 00:32:44,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then Mara will, the trapper, will still get to see you, because you left all the footprints around the bait and you just followed him back.

442
00:32:45,283 --> 00:32:49,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we'll compare that to the views that you would have.

443
00:32:49,047 --> 00:32:58,496
[SPEAKER_00]: When you read... when the views are mentioned in the suttas, people often think on these, like, metaphysical ideas about the world, about a life, about the universe.

444
00:32:58,556 --> 00:32:58,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

445
00:32:59,356 --> 00:33:04,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Views means standpoints and opinions that you feel blindly justified about.

446
00:33:06,072 --> 00:33:08,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes people say, oh, you shouldn't hold to fixed views.

447
00:33:09,213 --> 00:33:10,913
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a view that is fixed.

448
00:33:11,654 --> 00:33:12,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not a choice.

449
00:33:13,334 --> 00:33:14,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Views are not a choice.

450
00:33:14,794 --> 00:33:17,815
[SPEAKER_00]: But what views and how you hold them, that is a choice.

451
00:33:19,196 --> 00:33:22,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I will not hold any views, because views are always bad.

452
00:33:23,257 --> 00:33:25,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is your view that you just stated to me.

453
00:33:26,598 --> 00:33:27,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you contradict yourself.

454
00:33:29,198 --> 00:33:31,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, oh, you will have to hold the views.

455
00:33:32,665 --> 00:33:35,589
[SPEAKER_00]: But are those views, right views or the wrong views?

456
00:33:36,250 --> 00:33:41,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Are their standpoints and opinions authentic, transparent, not contradictory or not?

457
00:33:42,607 --> 00:33:44,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically speaking, views are anything.

458
00:33:44,827 --> 00:33:52,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything from... you shouldn't be wearing your robe like that, or monks should get up at six o'clock, or we shouldn't be doing this, or you shouldn't be doing that.

459
00:33:52,989 --> 00:33:56,890
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know whether you took the view or not, if by somebody disagrees with you.

460
00:33:57,410 --> 00:33:58,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel ill will?

461
00:33:58,310 --> 00:33:59,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel irritation?

462
00:33:59,391 --> 00:34:00,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you're holding the views.

463
00:34:01,071 --> 00:34:04,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's the purest views on the purest dhamma.

464
00:34:05,312 --> 00:34:08,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is the passion in regard to the standpoints?

465
00:34:09,233 --> 00:34:10,773
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to what should be done?

466
00:34:11,689 --> 00:34:15,950
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to how we should behave, is there real will if that's not met?

467
00:34:16,151 --> 00:34:17,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there irritation?

468
00:34:17,311 --> 00:34:19,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, that's why Trapper still sees me.

469
00:34:19,552 --> 00:34:20,552
[SPEAKER_00]: I left the trail.

470
00:34:23,493 --> 00:34:29,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, when I say not holding on to views, it's not like I don't have an opinion on what, how monks should wear their robes.

471
00:34:29,275 --> 00:34:29,695
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I do.

472
00:34:29,775 --> 00:34:30,875
[SPEAKER_00]: I have hundreds of opinions.

473
00:34:31,836 --> 00:34:38,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you disagree with that opinion, or if you prove that that opinion is wrong, my emotional state will be completely unaffected.

474
00:34:39,260 --> 00:34:41,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you prove me wrong, I'll gladly accept that.

475
00:34:41,882 --> 00:34:44,183
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just disagree because you're angry, I'll just see you as a fool.

476
00:34:44,343 --> 00:34:44,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Factually.

477
00:34:45,004 --> 00:34:45,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, I don't care.

478
00:34:46,545 --> 00:34:48,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I don't hold to these views.

479
00:34:48,626 --> 00:34:50,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean I don't have the views.

480
00:34:50,527 --> 00:34:51,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's another thing.

481
00:34:51,528 --> 00:34:54,930
[SPEAKER_00]: The same principle of, oh, the essential objects are a problem.

482
00:34:55,510 --> 00:34:56,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me run on top of a mountain.

483
00:34:57,391 --> 00:34:58,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the views are a problem.

484
00:34:58,672 --> 00:34:59,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me have no views.

485
00:35:00,846 --> 00:35:02,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly the same thing.

486
00:35:02,087 --> 00:35:03,908
[SPEAKER_00]: People do the same thing in regard to the views.

487
00:35:04,208 --> 00:35:05,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to not have them.

488
00:35:05,548 --> 00:35:06,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to never have them.

489
00:35:06,529 --> 00:35:07,909
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... but that is a view.

490
00:35:08,410 --> 00:35:11,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you're of a view that you mustn't have any views.

491
00:35:12,712 --> 00:35:14,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And... so you're contradicting yourself.

492
00:35:14,553 --> 00:35:15,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're inauthentic completely.

493
00:35:16,854 --> 00:35:16,994
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

494
00:35:17,214 --> 00:35:18,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Have all the views you want.

495
00:35:18,254 --> 00:35:19,115
[SPEAKER_00]: They were never the problem.

496
00:35:19,787 --> 00:35:23,529
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was basically underlying emotional state of that view.

497
00:35:24,690 --> 00:35:33,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, you have the view, the purest view, the cleanest view, and somebody comes and spits on it, mocks you for it.

498
00:35:34,055 --> 00:35:35,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel emotionally disturbed?

499
00:35:35,836 --> 00:35:36,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

500
00:35:36,257 --> 00:35:41,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, you are wrong to that extent for holding that view and that's why you're not free from sensuality.

501
00:35:44,161 --> 00:35:46,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, internally, you have not removed the passion.

502
00:35:47,870 --> 00:35:57,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's subtler, obviously, than indulgence and complete asceticism, but it still mirrors the same principle of sensuality.

503
00:35:57,619 --> 00:36:01,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do you like, do you relish the idea of people agreeing with what you said?

504
00:36:01,843 --> 00:36:05,486
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you're emotionally invested in your opinions and your views.

505
00:36:05,586 --> 00:36:06,627
[SPEAKER_00]: You're the third type.

506
00:36:07,763 --> 00:36:13,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel threatened emotionally with the possibility somebody will disagree or disapprove of what you said?

507
00:36:14,608 --> 00:36:16,910
[SPEAKER_00]: You are emotionally holding your views with passion.

508
00:36:17,430 --> 00:36:18,531
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the third type.

509
00:36:19,872 --> 00:36:26,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't matter the content of your view, the content of your opinion, the facts.

510
00:36:26,257 --> 00:36:26,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

511
00:36:27,578 --> 00:36:30,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you indifferent, equanimous in regard to it as a basis or not?

512
00:36:31,060 --> 00:36:32,041
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the problem is.

513
00:36:32,461 --> 00:36:33,862
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're not free from centrality.

514
00:36:35,183 --> 00:36:35,664
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's it.

515
00:36:36,535 --> 00:36:39,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to not have the views, just try to not be moved by them.

516
00:36:40,377 --> 00:36:46,921
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you will also not lose perspective, because you will not hold on to views now for the emotional protection, but you will actually be able to.

517
00:36:47,461 --> 00:36:49,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Wait a minute, I'm having these views, but are these views right?

518
00:36:50,743 --> 00:36:53,465
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you look and you find out you're wrong, and you will not be threatened.

519
00:36:53,805 --> 00:36:54,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people are threatened.

520
00:36:55,646 --> 00:37:02,910
[SPEAKER_00]: By experience, by hearing the opposing opinion on some important topic, they feel existentially threatened.

521
00:37:03,511 --> 00:37:05,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And this, you know, often you hear that.

522
00:37:05,664 --> 00:37:08,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, you know, this mustn't be said.

523
00:37:09,746 --> 00:37:10,927
[SPEAKER_00]: No, anything can be said.

524
00:37:12,667 --> 00:37:15,709
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the problem with, like, you're going to try preventing people from saying things?

525
00:37:15,809 --> 00:37:17,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

526
00:37:18,750 --> 00:37:24,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they hold on to their views so much, that just the thought of the opposing view fills them with anxiety.

527
00:37:25,714 --> 00:37:32,277
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, if you stop holding on to your views, not try to not have them, you get to understand, and then you actually get to explore.

528
00:37:33,308 --> 00:37:35,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And push the directions that you were afraid to before.

529
00:37:36,230 --> 00:37:39,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, that's how you also get to abandon the wrong views of a patujana.

530
00:37:40,533 --> 00:37:41,474
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be fearless.

531
00:37:42,755 --> 00:37:52,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Because fundamentally, you are not a sotāpanna, because you regard yourself, as the owner of yourself, as permanent, suitable for pleasure, all these things.

532
00:37:52,342 --> 00:37:59,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if these worldly views upset you, how much more you're going to be upset emotionally, if you start underlining your existential views?

533
00:38:03,053 --> 00:38:12,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's necessary to start seeing that there is no emotional justification for any view that you have, right or wrong, doesn't matter.

534
00:38:14,141 --> 00:38:17,423
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is an emotional response, emotional basis, emotional involvement, you are wrong.

535
00:38:18,604 --> 00:38:21,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can get upset on account of what somebody said, you are wrong.

536
00:38:22,267 --> 00:38:24,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if they're a complete idiot, doesn't matter.

537
00:38:24,989 --> 00:38:29,252
[SPEAKER_00]: From your point of view, from your point of... if you're interested in practice, you are wrong.

538
00:38:31,331 --> 00:38:37,436
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have emotional investment in any view, in any opinion, in any standpoint...

539
00:38:38,016 --> 00:38:39,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's obviously what...

540
00:38:40,178 --> 00:38:45,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how you become careful in regard to the views and standpoints that you have, that you have to have.

541
00:38:46,143 --> 00:38:48,144
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you learn to not leave the tracks.

542
00:38:48,624 --> 00:38:51,106
[SPEAKER_00]: And you become the fourth type of herd.

543
00:38:52,528 --> 00:38:53,688
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha had...

544
00:38:54,709 --> 00:38:59,273
[SPEAKER_00]: If you judge by the quantity of views, he had a view and opinion on everything.

545
00:38:59,773 --> 00:39:00,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma or non-dhamma.

546
00:39:02,264 --> 00:39:10,130
[SPEAKER_00]: But what he didn't have is passion underlying any of it.

547
00:39:12,111 --> 00:39:21,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So the fourth deer herd make their dwellings where the deer trapper cannot go and thus cannot be caught.

548
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:28,286
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those are the contemplatives who are both externally and internally restrained.

549
00:39:28,847 --> 00:39:32,671
[SPEAKER_01]: They did not act out of pressure, craving, and they developed jhāna.

550
00:39:32,811 --> 00:39:36,295
[SPEAKER_01]: They placed the place where Mara cannot find.

551
00:39:36,335 --> 00:39:41,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the only successful group.

552
00:39:42,061 --> 00:39:44,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is basically a refusing

553
00:39:45,096 --> 00:39:51,061
[SPEAKER_00]: To accept and give in and welcome and attain any external justification for yourself, for your standpoint.

554
00:39:51,862 --> 00:39:53,083
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you become the fourth.

555
00:39:54,084 --> 00:40:03,552
[SPEAKER_00]: See, the third type uses views and opinions about what should be done, sense restraint and virtue, all that's important, but they take that as justification, self-justifying.

556
00:40:04,453 --> 00:40:08,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they build their place, well, within the grounds that Mahārāja can enter.

557
00:40:09,818 --> 00:40:15,603
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you say, well, even that, the most obvious factual thing, you do need to be sensory-strained and keep the virtue.

558
00:40:15,783 --> 00:40:21,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my view, my opinion, that it's factually right if you want to practice the dhamma, but, emotionally, I'm not invested in it.

559
00:40:22,088 --> 00:40:24,870
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you build the shelter where Mahārāja will not see you.

560
00:40:25,571 --> 00:40:27,753
[SPEAKER_00]: You're invisible to the trapper, to the hunter.

561
00:40:28,934 --> 00:40:31,756
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the jhāna we spoke about in all the previous talks as well.

562
00:40:32,917 --> 00:40:38,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not acting out, learning how to endure first and then abide in that state of non-acting,

563
00:40:40,398 --> 00:40:44,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-standpoints, not revolving around this should be done, that should be done.

564
00:40:44,301 --> 00:40:46,062
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind does, you don't take it up.

565
00:40:46,162 --> 00:40:48,184
[SPEAKER_00]: You let it happen, but you don't act out of it.

566
00:40:48,744 --> 00:40:53,507
[SPEAKER_00]: And you are withdrawing from the mother's domain.

567
00:40:56,529 --> 00:40:57,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Invisible.

568
00:40:57,450 --> 00:40:58,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, become invisible.

